from full_a import C
LONG = {}

LONG["samson"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Samson was", """Samson was one of the judges of Israel, about 1100 BC. He came from the tribe of Dan and the village of Zorah. For forty years, Israel had been under the control of the Philistines, a strong people on the coast with iron weapons. The Philistines were the main danger in Samson’s time, and also in the time of Saul and David.

Samson is known for his enormous physical strength. But his story is also one of the saddest in the Bible: a man with great gifts who kept making bad choices.""", ("Judges", 13)),
("Why his story matters", """Samson’s story is about a person set apart by God from before birth, and how he nearly threw it away. It shows God’s patience, and that He can use even a flawed person to do His work.""")],
cards=[
C("A promised baby", "An angel told a childless woman she would have a son who would be a Nazirite and begin to save Israel.",
"""Manoah and his wife had no children. The angel of the Lord appeared to the woman and said she would have a son. “Therefore be careful and drink no wine or strong drink, and eat nothing unclean, for behold, you shall conceive and bear a son. No razor shall come upon his head, for the child shall be a Nazirite to God from the womb, and he shall begin to save Israel from the hand of the Philistines.”

A Nazirite was someone who made a special vow to God. He could not drink wine, touch a dead body or cut his hair. Normally it was a temporary vow, but Samson’s was for life. Manoah prayed that the man of God would come back to teach them how to raise the child. The angel came again and repeated the instructions. When Manoah offered a sacrifice, the angel went up in the flame, and the couple fell on their faces. The boy was born and named Samson. “The boy grew, and the Lord blessed him. And the Spirit of the Lord began to stir him.”""", ("Judges", 13)),
C("A lion, a riddle and a bad choice", "Samson killed a lion with his bare hands, then married a Philistine woman against his parents’ advice.",
"""Samson went down to the Philistine town of Timnah and saw a young woman he wanted to marry. His parents objected: “Is there not a woman among the daughters of your relatives, or among all our people, that you must go to take a wife from the uncircumcised Philistines?” He insisted: “Get her for me, for she is right in my eyes.” The Bible adds that this was “from the Lord,” who was seeking an opportunity against the Philistines.

On the way, a young lion attacked him, and “the Spirit of the Lord rushed upon him, and though he had nothing in his hand, he tore the lion in pieces as one tears a young goat.” Later he passed the carcass and found bees and honey in it. He scooped out some honey and ate it, breaking his Nazirite vow, since he had touched a dead animal.

At the wedding feast he gave thirty Philistine men a riddle: “Out of the eater came something to eat. Out of the strong came something sweet.” His bride was pressured by her people and pried the answer out of him by crying for seven days. The men answered. Furious, Samson went to Ashkelon, killed thirty Philistines and gave their clothes to the men. His wife was then given to another man.""", ("Judges", 14)),
C("A jawbone", "Samson burned the Philistines’ fields and killed a thousand men with the jawbone of a donkey.",
"""Samson took revenge by catching 300 foxes, tying torches to their tails and setting them loose in the Philistines’ grain fields, vineyards and olive groves. The Philistines retaliated by burning his wife and her father. Samson attacked them “hip and thigh with a great slaughter.”

The Philistines then came to Lehi in Judah, demanding Samson. Three thousand men of Judah went to get him, and Samson let them tie him with two new ropes. When he came to Lehi, “the Spirit of the Lord rushed upon him, and the ropes that were on his arms became as flax that has caught fire.” He grabbed the jawbone of a donkey and killed a thousand men.

Exhausted, he cried out to God, “You have granted this great salvation by the hand of your servant, and shall I now die of thirst?” God split a hollow place in the ground and water came out. The Bible says that “he judged Israel in the days of the Philistines twenty years.”""", ("Judges", 15)),
C("Delilah", "Samson fell in love with Delilah, who was bribed by the Philistine rulers to find the secret of his strength.",
"""Samson fell in love with a woman named Delilah, who lived in the valley of Sorek. The Philistine rulers came to her and each offered 1,100 pieces of silver if she would find out the secret of his great strength.

Three times she asked, and three times he gave a false answer: bind me with seven fresh bowstrings, with new ropes, or weave my hair into a loom. Each time she called, “The Philistines are upon you, Samson!” and each time he broke free. Yet he did not leave her. Day after day she nagged him, “until his soul was vexed to death.” At last he told her everything: “A razor has never come upon my head, for I have been a Nazirite to God from my mother’s womb. If my head is shaved, then my strength will leave me.”

Delilah lulled him to sleep on her lap and had a man shave off his seven locks. “And his strength left him.” She called, “The Philistines are upon you, Samson!” He woke and thought, “I will go out as at other times and shake myself free.” “But he did not know that the Lord had left him.”""", ("Judges", 16)),
C("Blind and bound", "The Philistines captured Samson, put out his eyes and made him grind grain in prison.",
"""The Philistines seized him, gouged out his eyes and took him down to Gaza. They bound him with bronze shackles and made him grind grain in the prison, work normally done by slaves and animals. The man who had once carried off the gates of their city was now blind and chained.

“But the hair of his head began to grow again after it was shaved.” The Bible does not say much, but the sign is clear: God had not given up on him.

The Philistine rulers held a great feast in the temple of their god Dagon, to celebrate. “Our god has given Samson our enemy into our hands.” They brought Samson out to entertain them. About 3,000 people were on the roof, and the temple was full of men and women.""", ("Judges", 16)),
C("His last prayer", "Samson asked God to strengthen him one last time. He pushed down the pillars of the temple.",
"""Samson asked the young servant leading him by the hand to let him feel the pillars supporting the building so that he could lean against them. Then he prayed, for the first time in the story, a prayer of real dependence: “O Lord God, please remember me, please strengthen me only this once, O God, that I may be avenged on the Philistines for my two eyes.”

He took hold of the two middle pillars and braced himself, his right hand on one and his left on the other. “Let me die with the Philistines.” He pushed with all his might, and the house fell on the rulers and all the people inside. “So the dead whom he killed at his death were more than those whom he had killed during his life.” His brothers and family came down and buried him in the tomb of his father Manoah.

Though Samson was flawed, the book of Hebrews lists him among the people of faith. He did what God had called him to do: he “began to save Israel.” Completing it was left to Samuel and David.""", ("Hebrews", 11))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Samson was gifted and foolish. He kept going back to the people and places that harmed him. The Bible shows that talent and God’s calling do not make a person wise. Character, daily obedience and humility matter a great deal. Small compromises, like the honey from the lion, led to bigger ones.

But the story also shows God’s mercy. At the very end, Samson prayed, and God answered. It was never too late for him to turn back, and it is never too late for us.

His strength, we are told, came from the Spirit of the Lord, not from his hair. The hair was a sign of his vow. When he stopped honoring the vow, he lost the sign, and without knowing it, he lost the relationship. The warning is to keep our hearts close to God.""", ("Judges", 16))])

LONG["gideon"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Gideon was", """Gideon was a judge of Israel, about 1150 BC. He came from the tribe of Manasseh and lived in the village of Ophrah. For seven years Israel had been under the harsh control of the Midianites, a nomadic people from the desert who came every harvest like swarms of locusts with their camels, destroying crops and livestock. The Israelites hid in mountain caves and dens.

God chose a young man who was hiding his own grain from the raiders.""", ("Judges", 6)),
("Why his story matters", """Gideon’s story is about God using someone who feels small, afraid and unqualified. It shows how God wins victories in a way that makes sure He gets the credit.""")],
cards=[
C("Threshing wheat in a winepress", "An angel greeted Gideon, who was hiding, as “mighty man of valor.”",
"""Gideon was threshing wheat inside a winepress, a pit in the ground, to hide it from the Midianites. Normal threshing was done on an open hilltop where the wind could blow away the chaff. Gideon was clearly afraid. The angel of the Lord sat down under an oak and said, “The Lord is with you, O mighty man of valor.”

Gideon’s answer was blunt: “Please, my lord, if the Lord is with us, why then has all this happened to us? And where are all his wonderful deeds that our fathers recounted to us?” The Lord told him, “Go in this might of yours and save Israel from the hand of Midian; do not I send you?” Gideon objected: “Please, Lord, how can I save Israel? Behold, my clan is the weakest in Manasseh, and I am the least in my father’s house.” God said, “But I will be with you, and you shall strike the Midianites as one man.”""", ("Judges", 6)),
C("Tearing down the idol", "God told Gideon to destroy his father’s Baal altar. He did it at night, because he was afraid.",
"""Before Gideon could lead, he had to deal with the idols at home. God told him to take his father’s bull and pull down the altar of Baal and cut down the Asherah pole beside it. Gideon took ten servants and did it at night, because he feared his family and the townspeople.

In the morning the town was furious and demanded Gideon be killed. His father, Joash, surprised everyone: “Will you contend for Baal? If he is a god, let him contend for himself.” Gideon was given a new name, Jerubbaal, “let Baal contend against him.” The Spirit of the Lord clothed Gideon, and he blew a trumpet, calling the Israelites to gather. Tens of thousands came. But Gideon still had doubts.""", ("Judges", 6)),
C("The fleece", "Gideon asked for signs from God with a wool fleece, first wet with dew while the ground was dry, then the other way.",
"""Gideon asked God for proof. “If you will save Israel by my hand, as you have said, behold, I am laying a fleece of wool on the threshing floor. If there is dew on the fleece alone, and it is dry on all the ground, then I shall know that you will save Israel by my hand.” In the morning he wrung out a bowl of water from the fleece, and the ground was dry.

Then he asked for it the other way: dry fleece and dew on the ground. God did that too. The Bible does not criticize Gideon for asking. God was patient with his fear. It is worth noticing, though, that God had already clearly said what He would do. The fleece was not so much about finding God’s will as about building Gideon’s trust.""", ("Judges", 6)),
C("Too many soldiers", "God told Gideon his army of 32,000 was too large. He reduced it to 300.",
"""Gideon had 32,000 men. The Midianites had 135,000. But God said, “The people with you are too many for me to give the Midianites into their hand, lest Israel boast over me, saying, ‘My own hand has saved me.’” He told Gideon to announce, “Whoever is fearful and trembling, let him return.” Twenty-two thousand went home. Ten thousand remained.

“The people are still too many.” God told him to take them down to the water. Those who knelt and drank directly with their mouths went on one side. Those who scooped water in their hands and lapped it like a dog went on another side. Only 300 lapped. “With the 300 men who lapped I will save you and give the Midianites into your hand.”

Gideon was now outnumbered by about 450 to 1. That night, God told him that if he was still afraid, he could sneak down to the Midianite camp. He heard a man telling his friend a dream of a loaf of barley bread that tumbled into the camp and knocked down a tent. His friend said, “This is no other than the sword of Gideon... God has given into his hand Midian and all the camp.” Gideon worshiped, and returned to the camp with confidence.""", ("Judges", 7)),
C("Trumpets and jars", "Gideon’s 300 men surrounded the camp at night with trumpets and torches hidden in clay jars.",
"""Gideon gave every man a trumpet and an empty clay jar with a torch inside. “Look at me, and do likewise.” About midnight, just after the guards changed, the 300 men surrounded the Midianite camp. On Gideon’s signal they blew their trumpets, smashed the jars and held up their torches, shouting, “A sword for the Lord and for Gideon!”

“Every man stood in his place around the camp, and all the army ran. They cried out and fled.” In the confusion the Midianites turned their swords on each other. The huge army fled across the Jordan, and Israelite forces from several tribes chased them. Gideon pursued and captured the two Midianite kings, Zebah and Zalmunna. The Midianites “lifted up their heads no more.” The victory was so well known that centuries later the prophet Isaiah called it “the day of Midian.”""", ("Judges", 7)),
C("After the victory", "The people wanted to make Gideon king. He said no, but he also made a gold ephod that became a trap.",
"""The Israelites came to Gideon: “Rule over us, you and your son and your grandson also, for you have saved us from the hand of Midian.” He said, “I will not rule over you, and my son will not rule over you; the Lord will rule over you.” It was a good answer.

Then he asked for something: the gold earrings from the plunder. It came to about 1,700 shekels of gold. He made an ephod, a priestly garment, and put it in his hometown. “And all Israel whored after it there, and it became a snare to Gideon and to his house.” Gideon had 70 sons from many wives, and one concubine’s son, Abimelech, later murdered his brothers and tried to make himself king.

The land had peace for forty years during Gideon’s lifetime. When he died, the people again turned to Baal. Still, the book of Hebrews lists Gideon among those who “through faith conquered kingdoms... were made strong out of weakness.”""", ("Judges", 8))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Gideon wasn’t a natural hero. He was scared and asked for proof twice. But God called him a mighty man of valor before he had done anything. God sees what He is going to do in us before we do.

The 300 men show us that God likes to work in a way that gets Him the glory. If Israel had won with 32,000, they would have taken credit. God used a small, odd force and made it clear whose victory it was.

Gideon’s story ends with a warning. He could resist being a king but still got caught by a golden ephod. Success can be a danger. A good start does not finish the race, and every generation has to choose to follow God.""", ("Judges", 7))])

LONG["matthias"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Matthias was", """Matthias is the man chosen to take the place of Judas Iscariot among the twelve apostles. He is mentioned only in the first chapter of the book of Acts, but the story of how he was chosen gives us a window into the first days of the church, just after Jesus rose from the dead and went up into heaven.

The Bible tells us only what is needed: he had been following Jesus from the beginning, from the time John the Baptist was baptizing in the Jordan, through the whole of Jesus’ ministry until the day He ascended.""", ("Acts", 1)),
("Why his story matters", """Matthias reminds us that God’s work is done by many faithful people who are not famous. It also shows how seriously the early church took the number twelve, and what an apostle was.""")],
cards=[
C("The twelve", "Jesus chose twelve men to be with Him and to be sent out. One of them, Judas, betrayed Him.",
"""After a night of prayer on a mountain in Galilee, Jesus called His many disciples and chose twelve of them, “whom he named apostles.” The number twelve was important: it matched the twelve tribes of Israel. Jesus was building a renewed people of God. He said they would “sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel.” They were to be with Him, to learn from Him, and to be sent out to preach with authority.

One of the twelve was Judas Iscariot. He kept the money bag and later betrayed Jesus to the chief priests for thirty pieces of silver. On the night of the Last Supper he led soldiers to arrest Jesus in the garden. Overcome with guilt, Judas returned the money, then went and hanged himself. The chief priests used the money to buy a potter’s field, which became known as the Field of Blood.""", ("Luke", 6)),
C("Forty days and a promise", "Jesus rose from the dead, appeared for forty days, and told the disciples to wait in Jerusalem for the Holy Spirit.",
"""Jesus rose from the dead on the third day. For forty days He appeared to His followers, spoke about the kingdom of God and proved He was alive. On the Mount of Olives, He told the eleven to wait in Jerusalem for the gift the Father promised, the Holy Spirit. “You will receive power when the Holy Spirit has come upon you, and you will be my witnesses in Jerusalem and in all Judea and Samaria, and to the end of the earth.” Then He rose into the sky, and a cloud took Him out of their sight. Two angels said He would come back in the same way.

The eleven went back to Jerusalem and went to the upstairs room where they were staying. There were Peter, John, James, Andrew, Philip, Thomas, Bartholomew, Matthew, James son of Alphaeus, Simon the Zealot and Judas son of James. “All these with one accord were devoting themselves to prayer, together with the women and Mary the mother of Jesus, and his brothers.”""", ("Acts", 1)),
C("A seat to fill", "Peter stood up and said that someone who had been with Jesus from the beginning should replace Judas.",
"""About 120 believers were gathered. Peter stood up and explained that Judas’ betrayal and death fulfilled Scripture. He quoted from the Psalms: “Let another take his office.” Peter set out the qualifications. The replacement should be “one of the men who have accompanied us during all the time that the Lord Jesus went in and out among us, beginning from the baptism of John until the day when he was taken up from us—one of these men must become with us a witness to his resurrection.”

The main job of an apostle was to be an eyewitness of the risen Jesus. That is what made the apostles’ message credible. The church put forward two men: Joseph called Barsabbas, who was also called Justus, and Matthias. Both had been with Jesus for three years. Both were qualified, and the Bible gives us no more details about either.""", ("Acts", 1)),
C("The lot falls", "They prayed and cast lots, and the lot fell to Matthias. He was counted with the eleven apostles.",
"""The disciples prayed: “You, Lord, who know the hearts of all, show which one of these two you have chosen to take the place in this ministry and apostleship from which Judas turned aside to go to his own place.” Then they cast lots. Casting lots was a way of asking God to decide, used in the Old Testament, for example in dividing the promised land. “And the lot fell on Matthias, and he was numbered with the eleven apostles.”

It is the last time in the Bible that lots were used to find God’s will. Soon after, on the day of Pentecost, the Holy Spirit was poured out, and from then on the church was guided by the Spirit in a more direct way.

Some people have wondered whether Peter was right to hurry and whether Paul was God’s own choice for the twelfth place. The Bible does not say. What it shows clearly is that the church wanted to be obedient and to pray first.""", ("Acts", 1)),
C("What came after", "The Bible does not say what Matthias did later. Tradition says he preached the Gospel and died for his faith.",
"""After Acts 1, Matthias is not mentioned again by name. But he was among the apostles on the day of Pentecost, when the Holy Spirit came and 3,000 people believed. When the book of Acts says, “Peter, standing with the eleven, lifted up his voice,” the eleven now included Matthias. And in the book of Revelation, the foundations of the walls of the New Jerusalem bear “the twelve names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb.”

Early church writers, such as Clement of Alexandria and Eusebius, say that Matthias was one of the seventy disciples Jesus sent out earlier. The Bible does not say exactly, but tradition has said that he preached in Judea and then in other regions, and was martyred for his faith. The details are unclear and differ from one account to another.""", ("Revelation", 21))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Matthias is a name most people do not know, and he did not give famous speeches or write a book of the Bible. What the Bible says about him is that he stayed. He was with Jesus from the beginning to the end and was ready when God had a place for him. Much of God’s work is done by people like that, who follow faithfully and never get a headline.

The way the church chose him is a lesson in making decisions: they used Scripture, they gave clear requirements, they prayed and they waited. In an important decision it is good to pray, look at what the Bible says and seek wise counsel.

And Judas’ empty seat is a warning and a promise. A person can walk with Jesus for three years and still turn away. But Jesus’ plan did not fail. God filled the seat, and the work went on.""", ("Acts", 1))])

LONG["simon-zealot"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Simon the Zealot was", """Simon the Zealot is one of the twelve apostles. He appears in all four lists of the apostles in the New Testament, but the Bible records nothing he said and no individual event. To tell his story, we have to look at who he was, who he was following, and what the Gospels show about the world he lived in.

He is called “the Zealot” in Luke and Acts. In Matthew and Mark he is called “the Cananaean,” which is the Aramaic word for the same idea: someone who is zealous.""", ("Luke", 6)),
("Why his story matters", """Simon’s nickname tells us something surprising about the group Jesus gathered. Jesus put together people from opposite sides of the political world. The story of Simon is about what happens when a person with fierce loyalties meets Jesus.""")],
cards=[
C("A zealous man", "“Zealot” may mean he belonged to a movement that wanted to drive the Romans out, or that he was passionate for God’s law.",
"""In the first century, Israel was ruled by Rome. Many Jews hated the occupation. They paid heavy taxes and saw Roman soldiers and pagan symbols in their holy land. Some groups wanted to rebel. The Jewish historian Josephus describes an organized party known as the Zealots who led the revolt against Rome in AD 66. That war ended with the destruction of Jerusalem and the Temple in AD 70.

The Bible does not say exactly what Simon’s nickname meant. It may mean he was part of a resistance movement before he met Jesus, or it may simply describe his burning passion for God and the law, like the Old Testament’s Phinehas and Elijah, who were called “zealous for the Lord.” Either way, Simon was a man of strong convictions who cared about God’s honor and about his people’s freedom.""", ("Luke", 6)),
C("An odd team", "Jesus’ twelve included both a Zealot and a tax collector, people who should have been enemies.",
"""Among the twelve disciples was Matthew, also called Levi, a tax collector. Tax collectors worked for Rome, collected the hated taxes and often took more than they should. Zealots saw such men as traitors. A Zealot and a tax collector would normally have been bitter enemies.

Jesus put them on the same team. Simon the Zealot walked, ate and slept alongside Matthew for three years. Peter was a fisherman. Others were brothers who fought with each other over who was greatest. The fact that they stayed together is itself a small miracle. It shows that Jesus’ way of uniting people did not depend on politics. What bound them was loyalty to Him.""", ("Matthew", 10)),
C("A different kind of king", "Jesus told His followers to love their enemies. Simon had to learn that Jesus’ kingdom would not come by the sword.",
"""Like many people, Simon probably hoped that Jesus would be the king who would throw off Rome. The disciples’ questions show this. Even after the resurrection they asked Jesus, “Lord, will you at this time restore the kingdom to Israel?”

Jesus’ teaching went in a different direction. “Love your enemies and pray for those who persecute you.” “If anyone slaps you on the right cheek, turn to him the other also.” When Jesus was arrested in the garden, one of His disciples drew a sword and cut off a man’s ear. Jesus told him, “Put your sword back into its place. For all who take the sword will perish by the sword.” To Pilate, He said, “My kingdom is not of this world. If my kingdom were of this world, my servants would have been fighting.”

The Bible does not tell us what Simon thought. But a man who had once put his hopes in a fight would have had to rethink everything he knew about how God would save His people.""", ("John", 18)),
C("In the upper room", "After the resurrection, Simon was in the upper room with the other disciples, waiting for the Holy Spirit.",
"""When Jesus was crucified, all the disciples ran away, including Simon. Their dream of the kingdom seemed dead. Then Jesus rose from the dead and appeared to them. Before He ascended, He gave them their task: “Go therefore and make disciples of all nations... And behold, I am with you always, to the end of the age.”

After the ascension, the eleven went to the upper room in Jerusalem and the Bible lists their names. Simon the Zealot is there, on the list, together with the rest. Then at Pentecost the Holy Spirit came on them all. Simon, like the others, was now part of the Spirit-filled church. His zeal was now directed toward telling people about Jesus.""", ("Acts", 1)),
C("What happened to Simon", "The Bible is silent about the rest of his life. Later tradition says he took the Gospel far from Jerusalem.",
"""The Bible does not say what Simon did after Pentecost. Later church traditions give several versions: that he preached in Egypt, in North Africa, in Persia, or in Britain, and that he died as a martyr. These traditions differ, and none of them can be confirmed. The Bible’s silence is itself part of his story. Many faithful people served God without leaving a record.

What we do know is that Jesus knew him by name, chose him, and gave him a place among the twelve. In the book of Revelation, the wall of the New Jerusalem has twelve foundations carrying the names of the twelve apostles. His name is there.""", ("Revelation", 21))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Simon was passionate. He was probably someone who cared deeply about justice and about his country. Jesus did not tell him to give up his passion. He redirected it. A zealous person has energy, and in the hands of Jesus it can become a powerful gift.

His place in the group is also a lesson. The church is the place where people of different backgrounds, parties and opinions can stand together because Jesus is their Lord. If a Zealot and a tax collector can share a table, the people of Jesus can learn to love each other across differences.

And the quiet pages of the Bible are a comfort. Simon’s name is written in the list four times and nothing else about him is told. Not everyone who follows Jesus is in the spotlight. But every one of them is known.""", ("Matthew", 10))])

LONG["james-alphaeus"] = dict(intro=[
("Who James son of Alphaeus was", """James, the son of Alphaeus, is one of the twelve apostles. He is always listed in the ninth place, and the Bible does not give a single word he said or a single thing he did on his own. He is sometimes called “James the Less” or “James the Younger,” possibly to tell him apart from James the brother of John, who was also an apostle.

The Bible does not say exactly how he is related to others, but his father’s name was Alphaeus. Matthew, the tax collector, is called “the son of Alphaeus” in Mark’s Gospel, so some have wondered whether James and Matthew were brothers. The Bible does not say.""", ("Mark", 3)),
("Why his story matters", """James is one of the people Jesus chose who left no spotlight story. His life shows that a person can be valued and important to God’s work without being noticed. To understand him, we look at what the twelve did together.""")],
cards=[
C("Chosen after a night of prayer", "Jesus spent all night praying, then chose twelve men from His followers.",
"""Luke tells us that Jesus “went out to the mountain to pray, and all night he continued in prayer to God. And when day came, he called his disciples and chose from them twelve, whom he named apostles.” James son of Alphaeus is on that list.

Jesus knew each of the twelve by name. He did not choose them because they were well educated or from the right families. There were fishermen, a tax collector, a Zealot and others. The Bible says that Jesus called them “that they might be with him and that he might send them out to preach and have authority to cast out demons.” Being with Jesus came first.""", ("Luke", 6)),
C("Sent out two by two", "Jesus sent the twelve in pairs to preach, heal and drive out demons. James went with them.",
"""After a while, Jesus sent the twelve to the towns of Galilee. He told them to take almost nothing: no bread, no bag, no money, only a staff and sandals. “And he called the twelve and began to send them out two by two, and gave them authority over the unclean spirits.” “So they went out and proclaimed that people should repent. And they cast out many demons and anointed with oil many who were sick and healed them.”

James was one of those who went. For the first time, the disciples were not just watching but doing. They came back full of reports of everything they had done and taught. James saw miracles done through his own hands. They were ordinary men who found themselves doing extraordinary things, because Jesus had given them authority.""", ("Mark", 6)),
C("Seeing the crowds fed", "James was among the disciples who distributed the bread and fish when Jesus fed five thousand people.",
"""Jesus and the twelve were tired. They tried to get away to a quiet place, but crowds followed them. By evening there were more than five thousand men, plus women and children, in a place far from any town. The disciples told Jesus to send them away to buy food. “You give them something to eat,” Jesus said. They found a boy with five loaves and two fish. Jesus gave thanks, broke the bread and gave it to the disciples to hand out. Everyone ate and was satisfied. There were twelve baskets of leftovers, one for each of the twelve.

James would have carried a basket of bread to the people. He saw with his own hands that a little, given to Jesus, becomes enough. It was a lesson the twelve would need for the rest of their lives.""", ("John", 6)),
C("Running and returning", "When Jesus was arrested, all of the twelve fled. After the resurrection, they were together again.",
"""On the night Jesus was arrested, His disciples promised they would never leave Him. But when the soldiers came, “all the disciples left him and fled.” James was one of them. The man who had followed for three years was now afraid.

On Sunday morning, the women found the tomb empty, and Jesus appeared to His followers. In the evening, the disciples were hiding behind locked doors, afraid of the Jewish leaders. Jesus came and stood among them and said, “Peace be with you.” He showed them His hands and side. “Then the disciples were glad when they saw the Lord.” For forty days He taught them. They were forgiven and restored.

James’ story is like the story of every disciple: he failed, he returned and he was sent again.""", ("John", 20)),
C("Waiting and going out", "James was in the upper room with the other apostles. After Pentecost, the apostles took the message out.",
"""When Jesus ascended, the apostles went back to Jerusalem to the upper room. The Bible lists them, and the ninth name is James the son of Alphaeus. “All these with one accord were devoting themselves to prayer.” At Pentecost the Spirit came, and the same men who had run away began to speak boldly. Thousands believed.

The Bible does not say where James went after that. Later traditions say he preached in Syria and Persia, and some say he was martyred. These traditions are uncertain. We do know that the early church was persecuted, and one of the other apostles, James son of Zebedee, was killed by King Herod Agrippa around AD 44. The rest of the apostles spread out across the Roman world. Wherever James went, he went as a witness of the risen Jesus.""", ("Acts", 1))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """If you have ever felt that you do not stand out, James is for you. Jesus chose him, trained him, sent him, and gave him authority. He is listed in the Bible four times, and every time it is among the people Jesus counted as His own.

God’s work depends on faithful people who show up every day. Much of the church’s work, then and now, was done by people who were never famous: someone who prepares food, drives a van, welcomes visitors, prays for a neighbor.

And the story of the twelve encourages anyone who has failed. They all ran away, yet Jesus gathered them again and gave them the work of changing the world. A past failure is not the end of your story.""", ("Luke", 6))])

LONG["zacchaeus"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Zacchaeus was", """Zacchaeus lived in Jericho, a busy trading city in the Jordan Valley, about 15 miles northeast of Jerusalem. He was a chief tax collector, which means he supervised other tax collectors and was very rich. He lived in the time of Jesus, about AD 30.

Jewish tax collectors worked for the Roman occupation. They paid the Romans a set amount and kept whatever they could collect beyond that. Many cheated. People hated them as traitors and thieves. Zacchaeus, the boss of the local tax collectors in a rich trading town, was probably one of the most disliked men in Jericho.""", ("Luke", 19)),
("Why his story matters", """Zacchaeus’ story is one of the best-loved in the Gospels. It shows Jesus seeking out someone whom everyone else had written off, and what real change looks like.""")],
cards=[
C("A short man in a tree", "Zacchaeus wanted to see Jesus but was too short to see over the crowd. He climbed a sycamore tree.",
"""Jesus was passing through Jericho on His final journey to Jerusalem. Just before, He had healed a blind beggar at the city gate, and crowds were thick along the road. Zacchaeus “was seeking to see who Jesus was, but on account of the crowd he could not, because he was small of stature.”

He could have given up. Or he could have sent a servant. Instead, a rich official in a long robe ran ahead and climbed a sycamore-fig tree, a tree with low, wide branches. It was an undignified thing for a grown man in his position to do. He was curious, and perhaps lonely, and he was willing to look foolish to see Jesus. Something in him was hungry.""", ("Luke", 19)),
C("“I must stay at your house”", "Jesus stopped under the tree and called Zacchaeus by name.",
"""When Jesus came to that spot, He stopped and looked up. “Zacchaeus, hurry and come down, for I must stay at your house today.” Jesus knew his name. He chose the least popular man in town. He did not ask permission, and He did not say He would come after Zacchaeus changed. He said, “I must stay,” as though it were a part of God’s plan.

“So he hurried and came down and received him joyfully.” The crowd was upset. “He has gone in to be the guest of a man who is a sinner.” In their world, eating with someone was a sign of friendship and acceptance. Jesus was publicly choosing to be seen with the town’s worst cheat.""", ("Luke", 19)),
C("A changed heart", "Zacchaeus promised to give half of his goods to the poor and repay anyone he had cheated four times over.",
"""The Bible does not tell us what was said at the meal. But something happened. Zacchaeus stood up in front of the others and said, “Behold, Lord, the half of my goods I give to the poor. And if I have defrauded anyone of anything, I restore it fourfold.”

That was a lot. The law of Moses said that someone who stole should repay the amount plus one-fifth. Four times was the penalty for stealing a sheep and slaughtering it. Zacchaeus went far beyond what was required. His giving was not demanded by Jesus, and it did not earn his salvation. It was the fruit of a heart that had already been changed by being accepted. Jesus had not lectured him. Love had done what accusation could not.""", ("Luke", 19)),
C("Lost and found", "Jesus said salvation had come to Zacchaeus’ house, and that He came to seek and save the lost.",
"""Jesus said, “Today salvation has come to this house, since he also is a son of Abraham. For the Son of Man came to seek and to save the lost.” Even though the crowd saw Zacchaeus as a traitor, Jesus called him a son of Abraham, a true member of God’s people.

The last line sums up the whole mission of Jesus. “To seek and to save the lost.” Zacchaeus was looking for Jesus, but Jesus was also looking for him. In the story it is hard to tell who found whom first.

Right after this, Luke tells the parable of the ten minas, to show that the kingdom of God was not about to appear immediately. Jesus went on to Jerusalem, where in a few days He would be crucified. Zacchaeus had met Him on the way.""", ("Luke", 19)),
C("What came after", "The Bible does not say more about Zacchaeus. Later tradition says he became a follower and a church leader.",
"""The Bible does not tell us what happened to Zacchaeus after that day. A later tradition, written by early Christian writers, says he became a follower of Jesus and eventually a leader in the church in Caesarea. The Bible does not confirm it.

But we can imagine the effect in Jericho. People who had been cheated were suddenly repaid four times over. The poor received half of his wealth. Neighbors who had avoided him for years had to decide what to think. A changed life is one of the strongest witnesses.

Zacchaeus’ name in Hebrew, Zakkai, means “pure” or “innocent.” It is a name that his reputation did not match, until the day he met Jesus.""", ("Luke", 19))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Zacchaeus shows that no one is too far gone for Jesus. The man everyone hated was the one Jesus chose to eat with. If you have ever felt that you do not belong, this story says Jesus knows your name and wants to be with you.

It also shows what real repentance looks like. Zacchaeus did not just feel sorry. He changed how he used his money and made things right with the people he had wronged. Faith in Jesus shows up in how we treat others.

And the story is about seeking. Zacchaeus climbed a tree to see Jesus, and Jesus said that He had come to seek the lost. If you are looking for Jesus, you are not alone in looking. He is also looking for you.""", ("Luke", 19))])
